# recz — project guide for AI agents

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with
code in this repository.

## Overview

`recz` is a Rust framework for working with regular expressions, distributed as
a Cargo workspace of several crates.

- **License**: MPL-2.0
- **Repository**: <https://github.org/quendimax/recz>

## Commands

```sh
# build
cargo build

# run all tests (unit + integration + doctests)
cargo test

# run basic tests (unit + integration), faster than `cargo test`
cargo nextest run

# run doc-tests only
cargo test --doc

# run only the unit tests inside src/
cargo nextest run --lib

# run integration tests that require std (map, set, vec)
cargo nextest run --test itest_std

# run the no_std integration tests (ordmap, ordset, vec)
cargo nextest run --test itest

# run a single test by name
cargo nextest run <test_name>

# lint
cargo clippy

# run miri (memory safety checker)
cargo +nightly miri test

# run code coverage
cargo llvm-cov

# run code coverage and open the report in the browser
BROWSER=firefox cargo llvm-cov --open

# run benchmarks (see recz-bench/README.md for the valgrind/AVX512 caveat)
RUSTFLAGS="-Ctarget-cpu=x86-64-v3" cargo bench --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-musl -p recz-bench
```

## Architecture

The workspace implements a regex engine as a compile-time code generator: a
pattern string is turned into a tagged NFA, determinized into a tagged DFA, and
then emitted as Rust source that is spliced into the caller's code via a proc
macro. Data flows through the crates in this order:

1. **`recz-syntax`** — parses a pattern string into a syntax tree and then an HIR
   (`Lexer`/`Token` in `lexis.rs` → `Parser` in `syntax.rs` → `Hir` variants in
   `hir.rs`: `Literal`, `Class`, `Group`, `Repeat`, `Concat`, `Disjunct`). It also
   re-exports `recz-codec` as `recz_syntax::codec`.
2. **`recz-codec`** — encodes/decodes text for the byte-oriented matcher (`Codec`
   trait implemented by `AsciiCodec`, `Latin1Codec`, `Utf8Codec`), since the graph
   below operates on raw bytes, not chars.
3. **`recz-graph`** — the NFA/DFA representation. `Translator` (in
   `recz-syntax/src/translator.rs`) walks the HIR and builds a `Graph` of `Node`s
   connected by `Edge`s (byte-set transitions, or epsilon transitions when a
   transition carries no symbols). `Tag`/`TagKind` mark capture-group boundaries on
   edges (a tagged NFA); `algo::determine` converts the NFA into a DFA `Graph`.
   `capture.rs` defines `CaptureGroup`/`CaptureLabel` (numbered or named groups).
4. **`recz-codegen`** — `CodeGen::build` drives the pipeline above (parse → HIR →
   NFA via `Translator` → DFA via `algo::determine`) for a given `Config`
   (visibility, haystack type, pattern literal), then `CodeGen::generate` emits a
   `TokenStream` of Rust code implementing the matcher/capture logic for that DFA.
5. **`recz-macro`** — the `__re` proc macro (re-exported as `re!` from `recz`)
   parses a string literal at compile time and calls into `recz-codegen` to expand
   to generated matcher code inline.
6. **`recz`** — the public-facing crate. Exposes the `re!` macro, `Label` (capture
   group labels, numeric or named), and the `str` module used by generated code.
7. **`recz-adt`** — shared abstract data types used across the above crates:
   arena-backed `Map`/`Set`/`OrdMap`/`OrdSet` (via the `bumpish` crate), `Range`/
   `RangeList`, `SetU8` (byte-set representation used for edge labels), and the
   `Step` trait. `recz-graph` and `recz-syntax` build directly on these types
   rather than `std` collections.
8. **`recz-cli`** and **`recz-bench`** — a CLI front-end and benchmark suite over
   the crates above; not part of the default build members for the library crates.

Because `recz` and `recz-adt` are `no_std` (see their `lib.rs`), avoid introducing
`std`-only dependencies into those crates or their non-test code paths.
